OTP restart strategies distinguish independent children from dependent siblings. A persistently broken child also needs a limit on repeated restarts.

> The point of letting an entire subsystem terminate and be restarted is that it brings you back to a state known to function properly.

Source: [Q01](/lore/durability-bibliography#q01); [B1](/lore/durability-bibliography#b1-erlang-and-otp-in-action), [B2](/lore/durability-bibliography#b2-elixir-in-action), [B3](/lore/durability-bibliography#b3-designing-elixir-systems-with-otp).

## Failure it prevents

Healthy siblings restart unnecessarily, or dependent siblings continue with stale state.

## Our Effect 4.0.0 reading

No OTP restart supervisor was found in the checked source. [Scope](/lore/scopes-own-resources) and `FiberSet` handle lifetime. Use XState for explicit restart modes and a host for runtime restart.

Source: `packages/effect/src/Effect.ts`, `packages/effect/src/Scope.ts`, `packages/effect/src/FiberSet.ts`.

## How to check

Generate child failures and repeated restart attempts. Check affected dependents, escalation, and reconstructed state. This check is our proposal. The books do not prescribe it.

Continue with [Restart must reconstruct known state](/lore/restart-from-known-state), [Circuit breakers have state](/lore/circuit-breakers-have-state).

Connect this to [the existing rat-stack guidance](/lore/lifecycles-are-machines).

